Z||000000||sdm1964||10-07-2000||07:20 AM||||I bought a trashed 86 GT last weekend to do a fastback conversion to my 86 SE. The Gt has rear defrost glass how hard is it to remove it? I would like to put it in the SE. Also going to put the Aux guages in my 85 GT. What are the wires in need to splice into to make them work?<P>tia<BR>Stephen||||reg||14
Z||000001||theogre||10-07-2000||03:58 PM||||Window is glued in. It won't be fun at all. there's some trim inside the sails that you can remove and get a bit more room. No idea what's involved to hook it up electrically.<P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-07-2000||04:22 PM||||Taking the rear window out is and royal pain in the butt. You know what I did, I went to "Advanced Auto Parts" and they had a rear window defroster kit on close-out. It wax $20 and had stips you just adheare to the glass and wire it to power and ground and it came with a little switch or you could hook it to the factory switch. Works real good. If you are still wanting to wire the factory defroster into your car I will look around tonight for some wiring diagrams. There at home, but I get them posted as soon as I can.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||10-07-2000||04:56 PM||||If you end up breaking the defroster window, I have one that I will sell. $10  + shipping<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||10-07-2000||08:36 PM||||There is also a timer circuit that you should get from the donor car. Haynes has the wiring dia. for the defroster.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Coop88||10-07-2000||08:38 PM||||I put a defroster window in my formula. I let my friend actually cut out the glass, after I had removed all the trim and the decklid hinges. The hinges aren't that hard to take out. He had a heck of a time cutting out the glass though. He does it for a living too. Be careful not to scratch the heating element while you're taking out the window. Otherwise, you could be doing alot of patching. Good luck.||||reg||10
